  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14095163/assets/3278eca2ae91f59c7c20d91f9190611291924dcc0cd6ffe74456552ba8400335.png)\n\nSince the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-2\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=-\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(-\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{arctan}\u20611+C \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & =-2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+C \\\\ C & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061x \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2(\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061x) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2(\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=-\\sqrt{2(\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-\\sqrt{2(\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}\u20613)} \\\\  & \u2248-1.1\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.",
